  "slug": "untitled-topic-f425",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers link elevation, temperature, and early-life stress to measurable differences in growth, metabolism, and development.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how environmental conditions and developmental context shape organismal traits, with local populations and individuals differing in measurable physiology and life-history outcomes. Repeated evolution is examined in deer mice across a gradient of native elevations, and prairie lizards from cool versus warm populations show local adaptations in thermal plasticity that reduce developmental costs at two incubation temperatures. Red-eyed treefrog embryos that hatch early under stress display altered tadpole growth trajectories and transcriptomes, consistent with life-history trade-offs. The avian study is less directly environmental: across 41 phylogenetically controlled species, longer-lived birds show lower routine mitochondrial respiration relative to maximal capacity. What is new is the explicit linkage of trait variation to ecological context across population, individual, and species levels. The designs are comparative and observational, so the items document associations rather than establishing causal mechanisms.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
